, and often contradictory discipline. After Gutenberg\u2019s press, it became a global, cumulative, and self-correcting system. This essay explores the origins of the printing press, its technological evolution, and, most critically, its profound impact on European science\u2014from the dissemination of empirical data to the establishment of universal standards for mathematics and physics.\n\n---\n\n### Part I: The Origins of the Printing Press in Europe\n\n#### 1. The Pre-Gutenberg Era: The Hand-Copied Manuscript\nUntil the mid-15th century, European science relied on handwritten manuscripts. This system had severe limitations:\n*   **Error Propagation:** Copying by hand introduced errors that accumulated over generations. A single mistake in a mathematical formula or astronomical table could corrupt centuries of subsequent knowledge.\n*   **Scarcity:** Books were expensive, rare, and accessible only to wealthy institutions (universities, royal courts, religious monasteries).\n*   **Inaccessibility:** Knowledge was siloed. A physicist in Rome had no practical way to verify the findings of a chemist in Paris unless they were physically present.\n\n#### 2. Gutenberg and the Birth of Movable Type (1440\u20131450)\nJohannes Gutenberg, a German inventor and printer in Mainz, revolutionized this process. While printing existed in China (block carving) and the Islamic world (woodblock printing), Gutenberg\u2019s key innovation was **movable type**:\n*   **Metal Type"}